<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Felipe Carrillo Puerto, Quintana Roo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chan Santa Cruz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Felipe Carrillo Puerto is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintana-roo">Quintana Roo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, in the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a>. Founded in 1850 as Chan Santa Cruz around a shrine of the Talking Cross, it was the capital of the rebel Maya during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste-war-of-yucatan">Caste War of Yucatán</a> until Mexican troops took it in 1901. It was later renamed after Felipe Carrillo Puerto, the socialist governor of Yucatán.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/felipe-carrillo-puerto-quintana-roo.jpg" alt="Felipe Carrillo Puerto, Quintana Roo" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leon Petrosyan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Felipe_Carrillo_Puerto,_Quintana_Roo.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
